    "passage": "The mandatory hijab law in Iran requires women to cover their hair and necks and wear loose clothing that covers their arms and legs. It has been [[1]] since the 1979 Iranian Revolution and the violators of this law can face fines, imprisonment, or lashings. Recently, there has been [[2]] opposition to the mandatory hijab law in Iran after the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini, who was detained by the “morality police” for violating the conservative dress code. Iranian women have been [[3]] of basic human and civil rights for over 40 years by a repressive regime that discriminates against them. The anti-hijab protesters have the well-being of Iranian women [[4]], demanding that the government respect their rights and dignity. A survey in 2020 found that 72% of Iranians opposed the strict and mandatory hijab. Recently, religious officials have called for greater tolerance toward women not wearing [[5]] headscarves. Even some fundamentalist clerics have advocated more tolerance in [[6]] the law, such as not forcing tourists to wear the hijab.\nUnfortunately, the current political situation in Iran has seen a resurgence of emphasis on the hijab law. President Ebrahim Raisi, an ultraconservative, has advocated for a [[7]] to strict observation of the hijab and instructed the morality police to increase their surveillance activities. Since the Islamic Republic of Iran remains tone-deaf to the plight and demands of the Iranian people, the [[8]] between the government and its people has deepened, and the divide between them has increased, with the hostility mounting before Amini’s death. Many Iranians, particularly the younger generation, who are increasingly [[9]] with the government’s repressive policies, are calling for a change. Now, the Islamic Republic of Iran is struggling to maintain legitimacy. With the [[10]] of stricter social and religious rules, there is no doubt that Raisi has made the situation worse.",
